[Full proof and inclusion table](./lowerbound_proof.md)

**The size–entropy lower bound survives composition under explicit smoothness, row-demand, and sharing assumptions.** If the \(k\)-th smallest fresh residual cofactor has log-size at least \(A\sqrt{\log N\,\log(k+1)}\) at the ranks needed for extraction, then solving a positive fraction of a batch costs at least \(L_N[1/3,(4A/3)^{2/3}]\) per target in expectation. The proof allows adaptive family selection, charges shared tests once, and requires no independence across targets.

**The exponent is tight for GNFS and MNFS.** Their classical cost models recover constants \(1.9230\) and \(1.9019\), respectively. I verified the MNFS optimum symbolically with rational filtering priced once.

The main arithmetic gap is sparse selection in high-rank modules. KH gives a positive constant for an explicit subclass, but extending it requires a bound on how many exceptional small norms supply distinct useful rows. For descent, tree incidence supplies distinct terminal leaves. Their residual cofactors still need a size–entropy bound.